<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mandume ya Ndemufayo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1894–1917</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mandume ya Ndemufayo was the last independent king of the Kwanyama, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovambo-people">Ovambo people</a> living across the border of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a>, reigning from 1911. He resisted Portuguese expansion, but after the Portuguese victory at Mongua in 1915 he withdrew into South African-ruled South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>. He died in 1917 fighting South African forces sent to remove him, and is honoured as a national hero in Angola and Namibia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mandume-ya-ndemufayo.jpg" alt="Mandume ya Ndemufayo" width="320" height="434" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mandume_ya_Ndemufayo_(before_1916).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
